\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The finite Wilson action and counting law}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the \vocabk{finite connection data}{fdip-006N} and a fixed real
#{\beta\ge0}, define the \newvocab{Wilson action}
##{S_W(U)=\beta\sum_{p\in\mathcal P}
 \left(1-\frac{\operatorname{Re}\chi_\rho(U_p)}{\dim\rho}\right).}
Since #{\rho(U_p)} is unitary, its eigenvalues have modulus one and
#{-{\dim\rho}\le\operatorname{Re}\chi_\rho(U_p)\le {\dim\rho}}. Thus
#{S_W(U)\ge0}. The action is gauge invariant and does not depend on
starting vertices or boundary orientations.}

\p{Use unnormalized counting measure on each edge group, and therefore
on #{G^E}. The partition function, probability and expectation are
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_W&=\sum_{U\in G^E}\ue^{-S_W(U)},\\
\mathbb P_W(U)&=Z_W^{-1}\ue^{-S_W(U)},\\
\langle F\rangle_W&=Z_W^{-1}\sum_{U\in G^E}F(U)\ue^{-S_W(U)}.
\end{aligned}}
These finite sums have #{0<Z_W<\infty}. An observable may be
scalar, or may take values in a finite-dimensional complex vector
space or in #{\operatorname{End}(F)} for the space #{F} of
\ref{fdip-006N}. The displayed sum is that
vector or operator sum, so the expectation is linear. The finite
group and chosen representation are inputs to this law, alongside
the square complex and #{\beta}.}

\p{The constant term in the action matters for the partition function.
With #{P=|\mathcal P|}, put
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Z(\beta)
 &=\sum_{U\in G^E}\exp\left(
   \frac\beta {\dim\rho}\sum_p\operatorname{Re}\chi_\rho(U_p)\right),\\
Z_W(\beta)&=\ue^{-\beta P}\mathcal Z(\beta).
\end{aligned}}
The two weights define the same normalized expectation. Replacing each
edge's counting measure by uniform probability measure instead gives
#{|G|^{-|E|}Z_W}; that is a different partition-function normalization,
again with the same expectation. In particular #{Z_W(0)=|G|^{|E|}}.
The action and expectation agree with the conventions in
\citet{equations (6.20)–(6.21), p. 8}{wingate2025lattice}; that source
uses normalized Haar integration for its compact-group setting,
whereas the finite counting convention here is explicit.}

\transclude{fdip-00M2}
